{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And you shall teach them diligently to your children.</b> That the words of Torah should be sharpened in your mouth — it is the language of sharpening, like (Proverbs 25:18) ״וחץ שנון״; and the intention is to say that if a person asks you something, tell him at once and do not stammer." "<b>And you shall speak of them.</b> That the essence of your speech should be in them — make them primary and do not make them secondary. 'In them' and not in other things; 'in them' and not in prayer — thus did the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expound. And the explanation of this is that words of Torah are in a loud voice, because Torah requires voice, since it was given in voice, as it says (Exodus 19) ״ויהי ביום השלישי בהיות הבקר ויהי קולות וברקים״ — but not prayer in a voice, rather in a whisper." "<b>And in tractate</b> Ḥullin (Psalms 58:2) ״האמנם אלם צדק תדברון״ — what is a person's craft in this world? He should make himself like a mute. One might think even for words of Torah as well — Scripture says ״צדק תדברון.״ And similarly Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 3:21) ״אל ילוזו מעיניך״ — since you see that they are the measures by which the world was created; and the second one (ibid. 4) ״אל יליזו״ comes to say: let not those occupations and distractions cause them to slip from your eyes; and therefore it says ״שמרם בתוך לבבך.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The citation 'ובמסכת חולין' introduces a Talmudic passage (b. Ḥullin 89a) built on Psalms 58:2; the parenthetical verse reference belongs to that Psalm. The parenthetical '(שם ד)' refers to Proverbs 3:4, but the phrase 'אל יליזו' is not found there; it appears Rabbenu Bahya cites it from memory or from a variant textual tradition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:03.343479354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}